+# The stance on water on the globe ground (Animation, 2026-11-20, a check; parked)
+
+The EP's item after GW1a: a fighter standing on the sea on the globe ground, read against Rendering's ground (the tile field), and whether the stance reads right in play.
+
+## Why a new check
+
+On the globe ground (`RenderTileGround.on`, the default since Orb's decision 3) a match is a planet slice and the sim's `S.water` is all zero: the sea lives in the tile field. `_test_water_stance` picks its water out of `S.water`, so it can only run on the strip ground (the GW1a pins say so). Nothing read the globe ground's own water.
+
+## What was built
+
+- **`render/anim/tools/water_tile_check.gd`** (standalone, headless, `--script`; `--strict` makes the finding fail): on the planet ring (planet 7, gen 3, the globe ground's own) at 240 places 640 units apart, the sim's floor with the water floor on (`WorldWater.floorAt`) against the tile field's drawn ground and water (`height_at`, `water_top`), the fighter's feet taken as the shared placement lifts them (`lift`). Gates: **sea** (the sim says sea at level 0): feet on the drawn water within 6 units; **dry** (no drawn water): feet on the drawn ground within 6; **wade** (the tiles draw water over what the sim calls dry): feet under the surface by less than `wade.below` (75) and never above it by more than 6. **lake** (the sim holds a lake's level) is a finding, not a gate.
+- **`_test_water_stance_globe`** in `anim_check.gd` (the globe twin of `_test_water_stance`): the match is the planet slice, the pair is put at a deep sea place, a lake place and a wade place with the floor on, and the body must read as standing (no airborne weight, both grounded, within 8 units of the floor). It leaves the floor and the ground switch as it found them.
+
+## What it found (planet 7, gen 3, 240 places)
+
+| Kind | Places | Feet against the drawn surface |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| sea (level 0) | 51 | on it: 0.0 at worst |
+| no drawn water | 149 | on the ground: 2.8 at worst |
+| wade (drawn water over what the sim calls dry: inland depressions below sea level, shallows, a lake with no level) | 23 | under the surface by 44.2 at most, under the 75 wade line: he is waist-deep, which is the rule |
+| **lake (the sim holds one level)** | 17 | **14 of 17 beyond 6 units: up to 59.2 above the drawn surface and 35.6 below** |
+
+**Does the stance read right in play?** On the sea, yes: he stands on the drawn water, grounded stance, nothing hovering or sunk (`_test_water_stance_globe`: deep sea, lake and wade all grounded, 0.0 off the floor). In a wade, yes as designed (shin to waist deep under the line). **In the lakes, no, as a sim-and-ground disagreement and not an animation one:** the sim keeps one level for a lake and the tiles draw the water at each node's own level (the drawn level of one lake runs 2,689 to 2,775 across its width on this ring; the sim's is 2,748.7 throughout), so a fighter standing on the sim's level can hover up to 59 units (0.8 body heights) above the drawn lake or stand 36 under it. The body's stance is right (it is a ground stance on the floor it was given); the floor is not where the picture's water is. That is World's lake level against Rendering's tile water, not Animation's: report only, and the check's `lake` line is where to watch it (`--strict` fails on it; on main's data it is a finding, on the GW1a stack it is the fault below).
+
+## On the GW1a stack: a real fault the check catches
+
+The same check on the GW1a stack (s422, `data/biomes/field.json` `everywhere` true) **fails**: `dry` 5 places beyond 6 (up to 32.6 above the drawn ground) and `lake` 17 of 17 beyond 6, **up to 681.5 units above the drawn ground** (about nine body heights), none below. `--verbose` lists them (lakes at x 22,400, 24,320 to 27,520, 49,280 to 55,040, 88,960 to 95,360). The cause is one row: put GW1a's `field.json` over main's data and nothing else, and the same numbers come back (no other GW1a biome file does; the water, contact, climate and ring files each leave main's result unchanged). With `everywhere` on, **the tile field draws no water at all over the lakes** (`water_top` finds none at the 17 lake places, nor at the 5 shallow-lake places that read as `dry`), while the sim keeps its lake levels and the water floor stands a fighter on them.
+
+**Read in play** (a hand-staged scene on the stack's real renderer, the idle pair put down at a deep sea, two lakes and a wade; no picture is kept, fighters' pictures stay out of git): the **deep sea** is right (both on the drawn water); the **wade** is right (feet in the shallows by the shore); at **both lakes the fighters stand in the air**, one over empty sky with no ground in view, the other against the buildings behind, with no drawn water or ground under them. The animation's stance is a ground stance on the floor it is given (`_test_water_stance_globe` is green on the stack, grounded and 0.0 off the floor at a deep sea, a lake and a wade); the floor is where the sim put it and the picture draws neither lake water nor a bed there. Owner: World (the lake levels) and Rendering (the tile field's water under `everywhere`); the fix is theirs, and this check is the gate for it: run it, expect `water tile check passed`.
+
+## Not shown
+
+- One planet (7, gen 3) and 240 places; other planets and sizes are not read. The check takes a planet and a step as constants.
+- Read from data, not from a picture: no clip of it in play (no fighter picture goes in git).
+- Not wired into CI (that list is not Animation's); `godot --headless --path . --script res://render/anim/tools/water_tile_check.gd` is the line.
